WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:04.660
Hello friends.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.149
This is Ashlee Vance,
the host of Hello World.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.430
And thanks for joining me for this journey

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.563
along the esplanade in Helsinki.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.750
This is a special VR
addition of Hello World,

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:21.060
so now is the time to put
on your fancy goggles.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.540
All this stuff was shot in 180 degrees,

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.520
with a stereoscopic camera.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.900
And we just wanted to give you guys

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.520
a peek at how we make the show,

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.800
while we were shooting the full version

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.713
of Hello World, Finland.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.003
Sunday morning, in Helsinki.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.090
The Hotel Kamp, right in the center

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:55.923
of the city.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.670
Here to interview an author this morning,

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.660
who wrote a book about the fall of Nokia,

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:07.170
to find out what went wrong with Nokia,

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.520
and maybe how Finland has recovered a bit.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:11.353
So this is a little

00:01:11.353 --> 00:01:13.600
bit of how we set up the interviews

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.250
that you'll see, throughout the episode,

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.690
for the camera buffs out there.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.360
You can see my director, David

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:21.963
Nicholson, nerding out.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.410
The art of TV hosting, I've discovered

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:29.270
here, is making small
talk with your subjects,

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:30.900
holding back what you really wanna ask

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.093
'em, until the cameras are on.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:35.263
I mean you know how the--

00:01:35.263 --> 00:01:36.230
Are you writing another book now?

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:39.260
I am working on, I just sold my next one,

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.660
yeah, so, I don't know if writing

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:45.120
has officially started,
but I need to be writing.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.787
I've got a little more.

00:01:46.787 --> 00:01:47.620
Can you talk about it?

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.286
Yeah, yeah.

00:01:49.286 --> 00:01:50.119
[Narrator] You can never promote

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:51.040
your new books too early.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.560
This was shot at a startup incubator

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.630
in Helsinki, this company is called Naava.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.550
And they make the coolest green

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.850
walls I've ever seen.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.460
Yeah, so this is a bio filter,

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.290
where we washed off the soil from plants.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:09.440
We placed in this biological filter,

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.610
which consists of seven
different ingredients.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:17.120
Some of them stabilize
the pH for the plants,

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.160
some of them keep the
humidity for the plants,

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.790
and the water and so
on, some of them provide

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.250
surface base for their microbes

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:25.590
to thrive and so on.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.270
It's kind of a combination
of different things.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:28.420
It took us like, two and a half, three

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:31.373
years to make this mixture.

00:02:33.388 --> 00:02:34.221
This is Maija,

00:02:34.221 --> 00:02:35.054
she runs Gold &amp; Green,

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:37.200
which makes a sorta meat replacement,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.020
that's really popular throughout Finland.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.050
The big theme in the middle of the episode

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.850
is food and all the weird science

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:47.900
that's going on in
Finland around new types

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.373
of protein and meat replacements.

00:02:52.278 --> 00:02:54.240
Before we hit up the labs,
there was this herring

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.380
festival going on.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.020
It's just right in downtown Helsinki.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.640
It was pretty cool, lot of action,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.323
and this mix of tourists and locals.

00:03:04.249 --> 00:03:06.332
Would you like a taste?

00:03:09.424 --> 00:03:12.174
I'd like to have a try.

00:03:13.507 --> 00:03:15.174
Thank you.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:22.990
This is Solar Foods.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:25.670
I think of it as like the Breaking Bad

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:28.550
version of making food in Finland.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.690
They make food out of thin air.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.120
They grab the air, they throw

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.683
it into some bacteria and cook it.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:39.140
As a TV host you have to give up part

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:42.763
of your control.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:46.953
And just be placed where they want you.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.160
This is at the headquarters

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:55.300
of Gold &amp; Green, with
Maija, who you saw earlier.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.810
One thing I found, with
the Fins, is that they

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.400
are really secretive

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:00.700
around their technology,

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.673
and wouldn't let me see it, half the time.

00:04:03.944 --> 00:04:04.777
You don't trust strangers.
Well, but also, kinda like

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:05.919
when you're a tech company, you want

00:04:05.919 --> 00:04:06.752
to protect what you have.

00:04:06.752 --> 00:04:07.790
So that part, I understand.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:11.540
But seriously, 'cause
I'll go to like, SpaceX,

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:16.420
rocket companies, or like
a fusion laboratory--

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:17.440
Like a self driving car.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.190
Yeah, there's definitely bits

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:21.220
where they're like, please don't show

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.460
this thing, but, it's just happened

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:25.970
now, three days in a row, I think

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:27.412
you Fins are up to something.

00:04:29.009 --> 00:04:30.030
Yeah, maybe that's true.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:31.090
We're here on the edge

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.013
of Helsinki in a trash dump.

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:36.150
Inside of there there's some zen robotics,

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.570
robots that sort out the trash.

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:40.850
Gonna head in, see 'em do their work.

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:42.191
And not get killed.

00:04:42.191 --> 00:04:43.024
Hopefully.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:53.898
If you're gonna visit

00:04:53.898 --> 00:04:55.200
a dump, this is the one you wanna see,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.943
because you got your garbage, you got

00:04:56.943 --> 00:04:59.200
your trucks, we got some robots

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.823
in this one, super fancy, AI robots.

00:05:07.272 --> 00:05:08.160
It's pretty cool, so the dump trucks

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.840
come and just bring this
whole pile of stuff.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.160
Kinda gets pre sorted by some humans,

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:16.840
and some other machines, and then it ends

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.780
up on this conveyor belt,

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.720
where the robots really go to town,

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.080
using AI to grab metal,
and wood, and plastic,

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.733
and sort it out into the right place.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:32.094
Something sorta
mesmerizing about the trash

00:05:32.094 --> 00:05:33.730
getting sorted into these piles,

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:35.550
and just going through the whole

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:36.713
conveyor belt line.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.200
Okay, this was my favorite.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.380
I'm here, with a future Finnish Olympian,

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:58.740
Lassi Etelatalo, whose
name I've learned to say.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.960
We were going to practice the ancient

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.123
Finnish art form known
as cellphone throwing.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:06.460
I was doing research for this episode,

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:09.060
it turns out that Fins
are into really weird

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.608
kinda competitions, like wife carrying,

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:14.050
and cellphone throwing.

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:17.710
They did this for years,
and our man, Lassi

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:21.100
here, was the actual
cellphone throwing champion.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:23.560
And this looks pretty
ridiculous, but I think

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:24.830
it sorta had a purpose,

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:28.090
which was a lot of our episode focused

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:31.090
on the fall of Nokia, that I thought

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.540
it would be a good idea to throw

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:34.840
some Nokia phones, to
help get into things,

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:36.350
and see if they held up.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:38.600
They're sorta famous
for being pretty sturdy.

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:41.530
It turns out they held up pretty well.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.140
Except I don't think anything can survive

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:45.190
this guy throwing it.

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:51.210
Wow, that was cool.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.043
This is me, doing

00:06:52.043 --> 00:06:54.100
my hosty thing, in a tram.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.490
The trams are a pretty awesome

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.880
part of Helsinki.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:59.320
My director David made me record

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:00.800
this in the tram.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:03.560
Little embarrassing,
because, I was bagging

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:05.873
on Finland a bit, and
they all speak English.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:12.620
These are the Allas pools.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:15.730
It's right at the edge
of downtown Helsinki,

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:18.167
they're really cool, you can go in this,

00:07:18.167 --> 00:07:19.040
which is like the regular

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.300
temperature pool, or you can go freeze

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.663
your everything off in
the Baltic Sea pool.

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.640
If you watch the full episode, you can see

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:29.810
me humiliate myself doing that.

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:32.370
Here's David torturing me, again.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:34.763
Telling me how to make
bubbles the right way.

00:07:36.084 --> 00:07:36.917
You know?

00:07:36.917 --> 00:07:38.258
I want you to create some bubbles

00:07:38.258 --> 00:07:39.422
around it, you know?

00:07:39.422 --> 00:07:40.791
Okay.

00:07:40.791 --> 00:07:41.902
Like that?

00:07:41.902 --> 00:07:43.523
Yeah, exactly.

00:07:43.523 --> 00:07:44.580
Even got Diana

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:46.593
here, who's a bubble coach.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:50.833
Bubble making partner, I suppose.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:07:59.833
That's it.

00:07:59.833 --> 00:08:02.850
Please watch the full
version of Hello World,

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:06.450
Finland, and leave us any tips for things

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:09.440
you'd like us to do in VR, or anything

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.623
we can do to make the
show better for you guys.

00:08:16.645 --> 00:08:18.562
More bubbles?

